From fbab0ed5debedd19513b5973b38e87da6db7dcdc Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: yekai pan <pyk@seas.upenn.edu>
Date: Fri, 2 Oct 2026 20:56:18 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] l2r-local: bug 19: give a cell's [value] record its own
 field-reference acquire and drop glue

`cell::get` and `cell::set` (ConvertToSTD) and a cell's drop glue reach
the cell's slot through a `field`-capability reference (a cell slot
projection must have it) and emit `ref.acquire` or `ref.drop` on it. For
a named record element that is not trivially copyable (a `[value]` enum
or struct with a counted member), AcquireDropExpansion outlined these
into calls of the record's acquire or drop function, whose parameter is
a reference of unspecified capability, so the call did not verify:
"'func.call' op operand type mismatch ... ref<...> field".

Make the capability part of the outlined glue, as the atomic kind
already is: `createDtorIfNotExists` and
`emitOwnershipAcquisitionFuncIfNotExists` take the argument reference's
capability, and a `field` reference gets its own symbols
(`drop_in_place_field`, `acquire_in_place_field`, and the `_atomic_field`
pair; RecordType::getDtorName/getAcquireName). AcquireDropExpansion
outlines `unspecified` and `field` references; any other capability is
expanded inline, as before.

The field glue exposed two places in `emitOwnershipAcquisition` that only
worked for a reference of unspecified capability: a compound's members
were projected as unspecified references (`ref.project` requires the
parent's capability), and a variant's dispatch arms took unspecified
references while the dispatch lowering coerces with the scrutinee's
capability (an unresolved materialization in ConvertToSTD). Both now take
the parent reference's capability, as the drop expansion does.

Inside a field glue body every nested `[value]` member is a `field`
reference too. The drop glue body already calls the members' glue (its
`ref.drop`s are expanded by the pattern). The acquire glue body expanded
every nested record inline (`emitOwnershipAcquisition`), which is
exponential in the depth of records shared in a DAG (`D(i) = {D(i-1),
D(i-1)}`) and would be paid at each record's glue. With the new
`callMemberGlue` argument, set by the glue builder, a compound member
that is a named record is acquired by a call to its own glue instead, so
the acquire glue calls each other like the drop glue. (The first,
inline, expansion phase before ConvertToSTD is still exponential for
such records; that is pre-existing and separate.)

For references of unspecified capability, the only kind that reached
these paths in programs that compiled before, the inline expansion is
unchanged. The outlined acquire glue of a record with named record
members now calls their glue instead of inlining it. The MLIR and LLVM
IR of Reussir's frontend tests (except the new test) and the LLVM IR of
30 lean2rr programs are the same before and after.

Compile cost: the cell part of the review's DAG repro (D(K) =
{D(K-1), D(K-1)}, get/set/get of a Cell<D(K)>) is now a call per site to
glue whose size is linear in K; at K = 10, `rrc --emit mlir-llvm -O
default` takes 4.6 s and 2.4 GB against 11.7 s and 4.6 GB with inline
expansion. The rest is the pre-existing first phase, which costs about
the same for the program without a cell (57713 lines of output against
83546 with the cell, whose free adds one inline value drop).

Found by lean2rr: an `ST.Ref`/`IO.Ref` of a `Nat` held in a `Cell` of a
`[value]` enum with a big-number arm did not compile (lean2rr now uses
two cells, docs/reussir-bugs.md, bug 19).
Test: tests/integration/frontend/cell_value_record (get and set of a
variant, of a compound and of a nested compound, a cell freed holding a
counted arm, -O default and -O aggressive; the field glue of the nested
record calls the inner record's field glue).
